BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Forestry Activist Greg King to Speak in Jenner
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260612T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260612T180000
UID:2605199T7u8HSkUkipFFHn69MW0Q
DESCRIPTION:Forestry Activist Greg King to Speak in Jenner\n\nForest Unlim
 ited Sponsoring Event at Community Center\n\nContact:\n\nCarol Sklenicka\,
  Jenner-Sheephouse Alliance\n\n\\(707\\) 865-0554\n\ncarolsklenicka@gmail.
 com\n\nMay 18\, 2026\n\nFor Immediate Release\n\nJenner\, CA\n\nForest adv
 ocate Greg King will speak about the history of logging in Sonoma County a
 t a fundraising event sponsored by Forest Unlimited at the Jenner Communit
 y Center on June 12 at 4 pm. King’s already classic environmental histor
 y\, The Ghost Forest: Racists\, Radicals\, and Real Estate in the Californ
 ia Redwoods\, published in 2023\, tells the story of his own radical activ
 ism—he pioneered the civil disobedience tactic of tree-sitting-- alongsi
 de the history of the rapacious harvesting that has destroyed 96% of the w
 est’s old growth redwoods.\n\nKing is president and executive director o
 f Siskiyou Land Conservancy\, a non-profit land trust in Humboldt County. 
 He is credited with discovering and saving the Headwaters Forest in 1987\,
  then the largest privately held ancient redwood grove still standing outs
 ide of parks.\\[2\n\nA native of Guerneville and descendent of the owners 
 of the King-Starrett mill in Monte Rio\, then one of the largest redwood m
 ills in Sonoma County\, King worked as a reporter for West Sonoma County P
 aper \\(now The Bohemian\\) with time out for activism to protect forests.
  To write Ghost Forest\, he did extensive research in forestry archives at
  UC Berkeley.\n\nThough he now lives in Arcata\, King is returning to his 
 home county to speak at a fundraiser for the Jenner-Sheephouse Alliance\, 
 a recently formed group opposed to a 1099-acre perpetual timber harvest pl
 an for the Sheephouse Creek and Jenner Gulch watersheds. The plan area is 
 partially surrounded by Jenner Headlands Preserve\, a 5630-acre property s
 aved from commercial development and timber harvest by Jenner citizens\, p
 urchased by conservation agencies in 2009\, and now owned and managed by W
 ildlands Conservancy.\n\nJenner-Sheephouse Alliance is campaigning for a d
 enial of the JB Berry NTMP that they believe threatens salmonid restoratio
 n in the watershed\, will increase fire risk by destroying tree canopy\, c
 ause sediment runoff into the Russian River\, and compromise Jenner’s do
 mestic water source. According to King\, “the threatened wildland holds 
 some of the finest remaining unprotected forest and salmonid habitat in we
 stern Sonoma County\, and it is a natural fit for addition to the Preserve
 .”\n\nTickets at the door for King’s appearance are $35. Those who don
 ate $100 or more will be offered signed copies of Ghost Forest. Doors open
  at the Jenner Community Center\, 10398 Highway One—behind the “C” s
 tore—at 3:30. Drinks will be available for $5 each and community club me
 mbers will provide free snacks.\n\nFor more information on Save Sheephouse
  Creek\, visit https://agora.co/campaign/save-sheephouse-creek or https://
 www.forestunlimited.org/category/events/
LOCATION:Jenner Community Center
URL:https://www.forestunlimited.org/category/events/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
